  "query_preview": "..\n\nWeak ridging conditions aloft will persist through Saturday. A \nwarm front is expected to lift through the CWA by this morning, \nadvecting above average temperatures and limited downsloping winds\ntowards our region. There is a chance of tying/breaking Friday's \nhigh temperature record in Glasgow (84 degrees - 2004) as \ntemperatures may peak into the upper-80s for some locations. This \nalso poses some elevated Fire Weather risk potential. High\ntemperature, low RH, will be conducive for Fire Weather in Zone \n137. However, winds themselves do not look high enough to provide \nexplosive fire growth over a long enough time span.\n\nThis afternoon through Monday evening: Anticipate a steep\ntemperature drop off tonight as a cold front moves through. Lift \nalong an incoming trough will begin causing rain Saturday night \nthrough Monday with a transition into a wintry mix possible at \nhigher elevation Sunday night. Trace amounts of snow will be \npossible, but accumulation at most locations other than the Little\nRockies are not likely with warm ground. \n\nTuesday onwards: Ensemble data sets begin to fall out of phase \naround Tuesday night decreasing confidence in the forecast over \nall. What little is starting to show is a weak ridging \nTuesday/Tuesday night for briefly drier conditions followed by \nanother trough wave somewhere around Wednesday night. Slightly \nincreased PoP chances over climo are expected Wednesday to \nThursday, but temperatures will be much lower throughout these \nperiods than the past couple days.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNO deviations from NBM occured. \n\nLow confidence in precipitation type Sunday through Monday.\n\nFIN/GAH",
